1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:03,660
Three hundred and fifty-five billion.

2
00:00:03,770 --> 00:00:06,565
That is how many payments carried Visa's

3
00:00:06,565 --> 00:00:08,690
name in a single year.

4
00:00:09,190 --> 00:00:12,740
Close to one billion every single day.

5
00:00:12,983 --> 00:00:15,071
And yet Visa does not lend you

6
00:00:15,071 --> 00:00:16,123
money.

7
00:00:16,123 --> 00:00:18,123
Neither does Mastercard.

8
00:00:18,143 --> 00:00:20,473
They did not even start as companies.

9
00:00:20,473 --> 00:00:21,819
They started as clubs,

10
00:00:21,819 --> 00:00:24,471
owned by the banks that used them.

11
00:00:24,717 --> 00:00:26,470
So how did two clubs of banks

12
00:00:26,470 --> 00:00:29,225
become the gate that almost every card

13
00:00:29,225 --> 00:00:32,580
payment in America has to pass through?

14
00:00:32,817 --> 00:00:34,999
By the end of this video,

15
00:00:34,999 --> 00:00:36,933
you will know how that gate was

16
00:00:36,933 --> 00:00:37,458
built,

17
00:00:37,458 --> 00:00:39,392
why it is so hard to go

18
00:00:39,392 --> 00:00:40,470
around it,

19
00:00:40,470 --> 00:00:42,680
and why the courts and Congress keep

20
00:00:42,680 --> 00:00:44,937
trying to pry it open.

21
00:00:50,583 --> 00:00:53,030
The story starts in California,

22
00:00:53,030 --> 00:00:55,113
in nineteen fifty-eight.

23
00:00:55,213 --> 00:00:57,671
Bank of America launches a new kind

24
00:00:57,671 --> 00:00:58,319
of card,

25
00:00:58,319 --> 00:01:00,483
called BankAmericard.

26
00:01:00,703 --> 00:01:03,243
The bank does everything itself.

27
00:01:03,243 --> 00:01:05,059
It signs up the shoppers,

28
00:01:05,059 --> 00:01:06,874
it signs up the stores,

29
00:01:06,874 --> 00:01:09,053
and it carries the risk.

30
00:01:09,293 --> 00:01:10,602
It is one bank,

31
00:01:10,602 --> 00:01:13,071
running one closed system.

32
00:01:13,317 --> 00:01:16,126
But one bank can only reach so

33
00:01:16,126 --> 00:01:16,967
far.

34
00:01:16,967 --> 00:01:19,005
So in nineteen sixty-six,

35
00:01:19,005 --> 00:01:21,753
according to the Federal Reserve's own

36
00:01:21,753 --> 00:01:22,647
history,

37
00:01:22,647 --> 00:01:25,395
Bank of America starts licensing the card

38
00:01:25,395 --> 00:01:26,517
to other banks,

39
00:01:26,517 --> 00:01:29,407
in the United States and abroad.

40
00:01:29,627 --> 00:01:30,652
That same year,

41
00:01:30,652 --> 00:01:32,754
a group of rival banks forms its

42
00:01:32,754 --> 00:01:33,779
own answer,

43
00:01:33,779 --> 00:01:37,007
a cooperative called Interbank.

44
00:01:37,067 --> 00:01:39,129
In nineteen sixty-nine,

45
00:01:39,129 --> 00:01:41,817
it takes the name Master Charge.

46
00:01:41,837 --> 00:01:45,135
That is the ancestor of Mastercard.

47
00:01:45,383 --> 00:01:48,953
The BankAmericard system was struggling.

48
00:01:49,083 --> 00:01:51,023
In nineteen sixty-nine,

49
00:01:51,023 --> 00:01:53,723
a banker named Dee Hock gathered a

50
00:01:53,723 --> 00:01:55,933
group of bankers at a hotel in

51
00:01:55,933 --> 00:01:58,743
Sausalito to redesign it.

52
00:01:58,793 --> 00:02:01,223
The answer was radical.

53
00:02:01,333 --> 00:02:04,069
Bank of America would give the system

54
00:02:04,069 --> 00:02:05,166
away.

55
00:02:05,417 --> 00:02:07,110
In nineteen seventy,

56
00:02:07,110 --> 00:02:09,468
ownership passed to the banks that issued

57
00:02:09,468 --> 00:02:10,457
the card.

58
00:02:10,457 --> 00:02:12,276
The network became a club,

59
00:02:12,276 --> 00:02:15,246
a corporation with no shares for sale,

60
00:02:15,246 --> 00:02:17,227
owned by its members.

61
00:02:17,237 --> 00:02:19,206
In nineteen seventy-six,

62
00:02:19,206 --> 00:02:21,175
after an internal contest,

63
00:02:21,175 --> 00:02:22,701
the club chose a new name,

64
00:02:22,701 --> 00:02:24,471
one that sounds the same in almost

65
00:02:24,471 --> 00:02:26,177
every language.

66
00:02:26,397 --> 00:02:27,699
Visa.

67
00:02:27,950 --> 00:02:31,120
Let me draw why the club worked.

68
00:02:31,250 --> 00:02:31,952
Before,

69
00:02:31,952 --> 00:02:34,106
every bank had its own card,

70
00:02:34,106 --> 00:02:36,284
and each card worked only in its

71
00:02:36,284 --> 00:02:37,610
own stores.

72
00:02:38,020 --> 00:02:39,198
Here is bank A,

73
00:02:39,198 --> 00:02:40,760
with its stores.

74
00:02:40,900 --> 00:02:42,117
Here is bank B,

75
00:02:42,117 --> 00:02:43,710
with its stores.

76
00:02:43,900 --> 00:02:46,710
Now put both inside one club.

77
00:02:46,830 --> 00:02:49,628
Suddenly every card works in every store

78
00:02:49,628 --> 00:02:51,520
of every member.

79
00:02:51,690 --> 00:02:54,224
Each bank keeps its own customers,

80
00:02:54,224 --> 00:02:56,817
but they all share one network.

81
00:02:57,083 --> 00:02:58,933
For almost forty years,

82
00:02:58,933 --> 00:03:02,213
that is what Visa and Mastercard were.

83
00:03:02,293 --> 00:03:05,143
Clubs owned by thousands of banks.

84
00:03:05,283 --> 00:03:08,067
Then the club turned into a company.

85
00:03:08,517 --> 00:03:15,433
If you like finding the hidden money behind the headlines, hit subscribe and like. It really helps the channel.

86
00:03:26,684 --> 00:03:28,954
Mastercard went first.

87
00:03:28,954 --> 00:03:30,689
In May two thousand six,

88
00:03:30,689 --> 00:03:32,953
it sold shares to the public for

89
00:03:32,953 --> 00:03:33,934
the first time,

90
00:03:33,934 --> 00:03:36,294
at thirty-nine dollars each.

91
00:03:36,394 --> 00:03:38,932
The offering raised two point four billion

92
00:03:38,932 --> 00:03:39,994
dollars.

93
00:03:40,074 --> 00:03:42,111
And most of the money went straight

94
00:03:42,111 --> 00:03:43,868
back to the old owners,

95
00:03:43,868 --> 00:03:44,607
the banks,

96
00:03:44,607 --> 00:03:46,735
to buy back their shares.

97
00:03:46,984 --> 00:03:48,605
Before its own listing,

98
00:03:48,605 --> 00:03:51,425
Visa in the United States was owned

99
00:03:51,425 --> 00:03:54,009
by about fourteen thousand banks and other

100
00:03:54,009 --> 00:03:56,254
financial institutions.

101
00:03:56,374 --> 00:03:57,812
In March two thousand eight,

102
00:03:57,812 --> 00:04:00,292
in the middle of a financial crisis,

103
00:04:00,292 --> 00:04:01,904
Visa went public.

104
00:04:01,904 --> 00:04:03,909
It sold four hundred and six million

105
00:04:03,909 --> 00:04:06,514
shares at forty-four dollars each.

106
00:04:06,514 --> 00:04:07,970
By our calculation,

107
00:04:07,970 --> 00:04:10,292
that is about seventeen point nine billion

108
00:04:10,292 --> 00:04:11,314
dollars.

109
00:04:11,434 --> 00:04:13,389
Visa itself calls it one of the

110
00:04:13,389 --> 00:04:16,162
largest stock offerings in history.

111
00:04:16,417 --> 00:04:17,647
Why sell?

112
00:04:17,647 --> 00:04:19,567
Part of the reason was the courts.

113
00:04:19,567 --> 00:04:21,687
Stores had been suing the card networks

114
00:04:21,687 --> 00:04:22,370
for years,

115
00:04:22,370 --> 00:04:24,254
saying the banks inside the club were

116
00:04:24,254 --> 00:04:26,267
setting fees together.

117
00:04:26,637 --> 00:04:28,077
As public companies,

118
00:04:28,077 --> 00:04:30,791
Visa and Mastercard would be businesses in

119
00:04:30,791 --> 00:04:31,606
their own right,

120
00:04:31,606 --> 00:04:33,667
not clubs of competing banks.

121
00:04:33,817 --> 00:04:36,413
Merchant lawyers even asked courts to undo

122
00:04:36,413 --> 00:04:37,662
the listings.

123
00:04:37,917 --> 00:04:40,137
Let me draw the change.

124
00:04:40,137 --> 00:04:40,800
Before,

125
00:04:40,800 --> 00:04:42,607
the banks owned the network,

126
00:04:42,607 --> 00:04:44,997
and the network served the banks.

127
00:04:44,997 --> 00:04:45,635
After,

128
00:04:45,635 --> 00:04:48,657
the network is owned by shareholders.

129
00:04:48,737 --> 00:04:51,017
The banks become customers.

130
00:04:51,167 --> 00:04:53,497
The network is now a business with

131
00:04:53,497 --> 00:04:54,872
its own interests,

132
00:04:54,872 --> 00:04:56,637
and its own share price.

133
00:04:56,884 --> 00:04:58,606
And it became a very,

134
00:04:58,606 --> 00:05:00,494
very large business.

135
00:05:00,494 --> 00:05:02,432
Because of a force that is almost

136
00:05:02,432 --> 00:05:04,971
impossible to compete against.

137
00:05:10,617 --> 00:05:12,044
In twenty eighteen,

138
00:05:12,044 --> 00:05:14,462
the Supreme Court described what a card

139
00:05:14,462 --> 00:05:15,777
network is.

140
00:05:15,777 --> 00:05:18,598
It called it a two-sided platform,

141
00:05:18,598 --> 00:05:20,384
serving two groups at once,

142
00:05:20,384 --> 00:05:22,537
card holders and merchants.

143
00:05:22,617 --> 00:05:24,493
And it said these platforms have what

144
00:05:24,493 --> 00:05:27,767
economists call indirect network effects.

145
00:05:27,767 --> 00:05:30,054
The network is worth more to stores

146
00:05:30,054 --> 00:05:32,316
when more shoppers carry its cards,

147
00:05:32,316 --> 00:05:34,348
and worth more to shoppers when more

148
00:05:34,348 --> 00:05:36,219
stores accept them.

149
00:05:36,484 --> 00:05:38,974
That creates a loop.

150
00:05:39,144 --> 00:05:41,704
More cards bring more stores.

151
00:05:41,704 --> 00:05:44,164
More stores bring more cards.

152
00:05:44,544 --> 00:05:47,468
The Court also noted that almost every

153
00:05:47,468 --> 00:05:49,904
bank offering credit cards is in the

154
00:05:49,904 --> 00:05:52,072
Visa or Mastercard network,

155
00:05:52,072 --> 00:05:55,596
because both began as bank cooperatives.

156
00:05:55,850 --> 00:05:57,701
Look at the size of that loop

157
00:05:57,701 --> 00:05:58,830
today.

158
00:05:58,970 --> 00:06:01,275
Visa reports five billion payment

159
00:06:01,275 --> 00:06:02,275
credentials,

160
00:06:02,275 --> 00:06:04,836
more than four hundred million places that

161
00:06:04,836 --> 00:06:05,835
accept them,

162
00:06:05,835 --> 00:06:08,397
and eighteen trillion dollars in total

163
00:06:08,397 --> 00:06:10,790
volume in its latest year.

164
00:06:11,110 --> 00:06:13,969
Mastercard reported ten point six trillion

165
00:06:13,969 --> 00:06:16,308
dollars in gross volume in twenty

166
00:06:16,308 --> 00:06:17,688
twenty-five.

167
00:06:17,950 --> 00:06:20,383
Among the big card networks,

168
00:06:20,383 --> 00:06:22,830
the gap is enormous.

169
00:06:22,990 --> 00:06:24,998
In Visa's annual report,

170
00:06:24,998 --> 00:06:27,005
citing industry data,

171
00:06:27,005 --> 00:06:29,546
Visa handled about fourteen point six

172
00:06:29,546 --> 00:06:32,088
trillion dollars in payments in twenty

173
00:06:32,088 --> 00:06:33,450
twenty-five.

174
00:06:33,570 --> 00:06:34,489
Mastercard,

175
00:06:34,489 --> 00:06:37,210
about eight point eight trillion dollars.

176
00:06:37,250 --> 00:06:38,897
American Express,

177
00:06:38,897 --> 00:06:39,828
the next one,

178
00:06:39,828 --> 00:06:42,576
about one point nine trillion dollars.

179
00:06:42,817 --> 00:06:44,877
Let me draw the moat.

180
00:06:45,047 --> 00:06:46,720
Here is a new network,

181
00:06:46,720 --> 00:06:48,047
trying to start.

182
00:06:48,117 --> 00:06:49,701
No store will accept it,

183
00:06:49,701 --> 00:06:52,367
because nobody carries the card.

184
00:06:52,547 --> 00:06:54,556
Nobody will carry the card,

185
00:06:54,556 --> 00:06:56,737
because no store accepts it.

186
00:06:57,107 --> 00:06:58,514
On the other side,

187
00:06:58,514 --> 00:07:00,159
Visa and Mastercard,

188
00:07:00,159 --> 00:07:02,305
with billions of cards and hundreds of

189
00:07:02,305 --> 00:07:04,097
millions of stores.

190
00:07:04,297 --> 00:07:06,130
The loop that made them big is

191
00:07:06,130 --> 00:07:08,825
the same loop that keeps others out.

192
00:07:09,084 --> 00:07:11,570
But a moat can also be dug

193
00:07:11,570 --> 00:07:13,724
deeper on purpose.

194
00:07:13,744 --> 00:07:16,091
And that is where the government stepped

195
00:07:16,091 --> 00:07:16,926
in.

196
00:07:22,584 --> 00:07:25,293
By the late nineteen nineties,

197
00:07:25,293 --> 00:07:28,303
Visa and Mastercard had rules for their

198
00:07:28,303 --> 00:07:29,704
member banks.

199
00:07:29,704 --> 00:07:31,563
A bank could issue their cards,

200
00:07:31,563 --> 00:07:34,527
but it could not issue American Express

201
00:07:34,527 --> 00:07:36,474
or Discover cards.

202
00:07:36,654 --> 00:07:38,644
The government sued.

203
00:07:38,874 --> 00:07:40,469
According to its brief,

204
00:07:40,469 --> 00:07:43,011
Visa and Mastercard then accounted for

205
00:07:43,011 --> 00:07:45,785
seventy-three percent of general purpose

206
00:07:45,785 --> 00:07:46,918
card transactions,

207
00:07:46,918 --> 00:07:49,829
and eighty-five percent of cards issued.

208
00:07:50,084 --> 00:07:53,004
The courts agreed with the government.

209
00:07:53,074 --> 00:07:55,604
The rules had worked as intended.

210
00:07:55,724 --> 00:07:58,620
No bank in the continental United States

211
00:07:58,620 --> 00:08:01,071
had agreed to issue American Express

212
00:08:01,071 --> 00:08:01,894
cards.

213
00:08:02,084 --> 00:08:04,920
Visa and Mastercard were ordered to repeal

214
00:08:04,920 --> 00:08:05,334
them,

215
00:08:05,334 --> 00:08:06,839
and in two thousand three,

216
00:08:06,839 --> 00:08:09,402
the appeals court upheld that order.

217
00:08:09,650 --> 00:08:13,050
Then Congress went after debit cards.

218
00:08:13,260 --> 00:08:15,438
A law passed in twenty ten,

219
00:08:15,438 --> 00:08:17,615
known as the Durbin Amendment,

220
00:08:17,615 --> 00:08:20,551
told the Federal Reserve to limit debit

221
00:08:20,551 --> 00:08:22,130
fees for large banks.

222
00:08:22,330 --> 00:08:24,582
Today the cap is twenty-one cents,

223
00:08:24,582 --> 00:08:26,859
plus five hundredths of a percent of

224
00:08:26,859 --> 00:08:27,846
the purchase,

225
00:08:27,846 --> 00:08:30,470
plus one cent for fraud prevention.

226
00:08:30,960 --> 00:08:33,747
The law also requires every debit card

227
00:08:33,747 --> 00:08:35,892
to work on at least two unrelated

228
00:08:35,892 --> 00:08:36,920
networks.

229
00:08:37,184 --> 00:08:38,985
In twenty twenty-three,

230
00:08:38,985 --> 00:08:42,194
the Fed proposed lowering that cap.

231
00:08:42,304 --> 00:08:44,961
On an average fifty dollar purchase,

232
00:08:44,961 --> 00:08:46,971
it would fall from twenty-four and a

233
00:08:46,971 --> 00:08:49,804
half cents to seventeen point seven cents.

234
00:08:50,034 --> 00:08:52,904
That proposal has still not become final.

235
00:08:53,054 --> 00:08:53,977
Meanwhile,

236
00:08:53,977 --> 00:08:56,344
two federal courts reached opposite

237
00:08:56,344 --> 00:08:58,948
decisions on whether the cap itself is

238
00:08:58,948 --> 00:08:59,634
valid,

239
00:08:59,634 --> 00:09:02,128
and for now it stays in effect.

240
00:09:02,384 --> 00:09:04,634
Europe went further.

241
00:09:04,634 --> 00:09:06,046
Since twenty fifteen,

242
00:09:06,046 --> 00:09:08,679
European Union law has capped the fee

243
00:09:08,679 --> 00:09:11,072
banks earn on consumer cards at zero

244
00:09:11,072 --> 00:09:13,466
point two percent for debit and zero

245
00:09:13,466 --> 00:09:15,741
point three percent for credit.

246
00:09:15,984 --> 00:09:18,040
Let me draw the walls of the

247
00:09:18,040 --> 00:09:18,934
gate.

248
00:09:19,154 --> 00:09:19,625
First,

249
00:09:19,625 --> 00:09:21,833
the exclusivity rules,

250
00:09:21,833 --> 00:09:23,674
torn down by the courts.

251
00:09:23,744 --> 00:09:24,412
Second,

252
00:09:24,412 --> 00:09:27,153
the debit cap and the two-network rule,

253
00:09:27,153 --> 00:09:28,664
set by Congress.

254
00:09:28,664 --> 00:09:29,199
Third,

255
00:09:29,199 --> 00:09:30,924
Europe's caps.

256
00:09:30,934 --> 00:09:33,004
Each one opened a small door.

257
00:09:33,004 --> 00:09:35,167
None of them removed the gate.

258
00:09:35,417 --> 00:09:38,341
And now the gate itself is on

259
00:09:38,341 --> 00:09:39,307
trial.

260
00:09:44,950 --> 00:09:47,318
In September twenty twenty-four,

261
00:09:47,318 --> 00:09:50,070
the Justice Department sued Visa.

262
00:09:50,300 --> 00:09:52,864
It alleges that Visa illegally protected a

263
00:09:52,864 --> 00:09:54,660
monopoly in debit.

264
00:09:54,920 --> 00:09:56,674
According to the complaint,

265
00:09:56,674 --> 00:09:58,671
more than sixty percent of debit

266
00:09:58,671 --> 00:10:01,335
transactions in America run on Visa's

267
00:10:01,335 --> 00:10:01,979
network,

268
00:10:01,979 --> 00:10:04,198
letting it charge more than seven billion

269
00:10:04,198 --> 00:10:06,130
dollars in fees a year.

270
00:10:06,280 --> 00:10:08,400
These are allegations.

271
00:10:08,630 --> 00:10:10,372
In twenty twenty-five,

272
00:10:10,372 --> 00:10:13,123
a judge refused to dismiss the case,

273
00:10:13,123 --> 00:10:15,238
and it is still going on.

274
00:10:15,484 --> 00:10:17,860
The complaint even quotes Visa's own

275
00:10:17,860 --> 00:10:20,474
description of the network effects around

276
00:10:20,474 --> 00:10:22,024
its business.

277
00:10:22,534 --> 00:10:24,662
An enormous moat.

278
00:10:24,917 --> 00:10:26,894
The stores have their own case,

279
00:10:26,894 --> 00:10:29,147
and it has run for about twenty

280
00:10:29,147 --> 00:10:29,997
years.

281
00:10:30,067 --> 00:10:31,357
In twenty nineteen,

282
00:10:31,357 --> 00:10:33,413
a court approved a settlement for the

283
00:10:33,413 --> 00:10:34,387
merchants.

284
00:10:34,527 --> 00:10:35,895
By our arithmetic,

285
00:10:35,895 --> 00:10:38,473
after money returned for stores that opted

286
00:10:38,473 --> 00:10:38,849
out,

287
00:10:38,849 --> 00:10:40,436
it came to about five and a

288
00:10:40,436 --> 00:10:41,997
half billion dollars.

289
00:10:41,997 --> 00:10:43,097
A second deal,

290
00:10:43,097 --> 00:10:44,647
in twenty twenty-four,

291
00:10:44,647 --> 00:10:46,818
was rejected by the judge.

292
00:10:47,084 --> 00:10:49,571
A new deal was signed in November

293
00:10:49,571 --> 00:10:51,414
twenty twenty-five.

294
00:10:51,434 --> 00:10:53,856
It would cut the average credit card

295
00:10:53,856 --> 00:10:56,035
fee by a tenth of a percentage

296
00:10:56,035 --> 00:10:57,604
point for five years.

297
00:10:57,754 --> 00:11:00,188
And stores could choose to refuse premium

298
00:11:00,188 --> 00:11:00,650
cards,

299
00:11:00,650 --> 00:11:03,684
while still accepting standard ones.

300
00:11:03,924 --> 00:11:06,500
It won preliminary approval in twenty

301
00:11:06,500 --> 00:11:07,694
twenty-six.

302
00:11:07,814 --> 00:11:10,723
Final approval is still pending.

303
00:11:10,984 --> 00:11:12,090
And in Congress,

304
00:11:12,090 --> 00:11:14,798
a bill called the Credit Card Competition

305
00:11:14,798 --> 00:11:17,507
Act was reintroduced in January twenty

306
00:11:17,507 --> 00:11:18,784
twenty-six.

307
00:11:18,934 --> 00:11:21,147
Banks with more than one hundred billion

308
00:11:21,147 --> 00:11:23,606
dollars in assets would have to offer

309
00:11:23,606 --> 00:11:26,041
a second network on credit cards,

310
00:11:26,041 --> 00:11:28,854
one outside Visa and Mastercard.

311
00:11:29,024 --> 00:11:30,694
It has not passed.

312
00:11:30,814 --> 00:11:33,134
Visa itself says it is unclear whether

313
00:11:33,134 --> 00:11:35,422
the bill will gain enough traction.

314
00:11:35,684 --> 00:11:38,150
Let me draw where the pressure comes

315
00:11:38,150 --> 00:11:39,024
from.

316
00:11:39,104 --> 00:11:40,519
The Justice Department,

317
00:11:40,519 --> 00:11:41,734
on debit.

318
00:11:41,794 --> 00:11:42,862
The stores,

319
00:11:42,862 --> 00:11:44,284
through the courts.

320
00:11:44,314 --> 00:11:44,952
Congress,

321
00:11:44,952 --> 00:11:47,534
with a second network for credit.

322
00:11:47,604 --> 00:11:50,624
Three forces pushing on the same gate.

323
00:11:50,764 --> 00:11:51,776
So far,

324
00:11:51,776 --> 00:11:54,471
the gate is still standing.

325
00:11:54,717 --> 00:11:58,039
So how did Visa and Mastercard become

326
00:11:58,039 --> 00:11:59,847
gatekeepers?

327
00:11:59,927 --> 00:12:01,590
Not by lending money,

328
00:12:01,590 --> 00:12:03,877
and not by owning stores.

329
00:12:04,017 --> 00:12:06,277
They became the meeting point.

330
00:12:06,397 --> 00:12:08,617
First as clubs that let thousands of

331
00:12:08,617 --> 00:12:10,327
banks act like one.

332
00:12:10,487 --> 00:12:12,834
Then as companies sitting on a loop

333
00:12:12,834 --> 00:12:15,180
that gets stronger every time someone

334
00:12:15,180 --> 00:12:17,292
opens a card or a store accepts

335
00:12:17,292 --> 00:12:18,127
one.

336
00:12:18,297 --> 00:12:20,867
That is the real business.

337
00:12:20,917 --> 00:12:22,257
Not the plastic,

338
00:12:22,257 --> 00:12:24,720
but the place where everyone has to

339
00:12:24,720 --> 00:12:25,593
meet.

340
00:12:25,850 --> 00:12:28,341
Next time on The Hidden Economics,

341
00:12:28,341 --> 00:12:31,312
we follow the money behind another hidden

342
00:12:31,312 --> 00:12:33,740
link of the world economy.

343
00:12:33,910 --> 00:12:36,476
This is The Hidden Economics.
